A makefile in Curry
===================

Make executes, Sprite computes.  This example builds a toy C project: four
sources, two headers, a static library of two objects, and a program.  Its
makefile is a Curry module beside the sources, project/Makefile.curry.  The
variables of the makefile are definitions, and each equation of the function
rule is a rule.  The library Make.curry asks the questions of make over that
function: what a target reads, what is stale, what to build and in which
order, what can run now, what a change touches, which targets two rules
claim, and which sources are missing.  The driver make.py owns the world: it
scans the includes, reads the stamps of the files, runs the commands in a
thread pool, and prints the status.  Nothing in make.py names a file of the
project.  The include scan is its one piece of knowledge of the language, so
the driver is generic for a C project.  Sprite itself builds with configure
and GNU make; this example does not replace them, and its rules are not
Sprite's.


1. What the example shows about functional logic programming
-------------------------------------------------------------

The makefile.  This is project/Makefile.curry, with its comments shortened:

    module Makefile where

    import Make

    -- The variables.
    cc :: String
    cc = "cc"

    cflags :: String
    cflags = "-O2 -Wall"

    ar :: String
    ar = "ar"

    library :: Target
    library = "libgeom.a"

    program :: Target
    program = "demo"

    libobjs, mainobjs :: [Target]
    libobjs  = ["vec.o", "shape.o"]
    mainobjs = ["main.o", "report.o"]

    -- The default goal.
    goal :: Target
    goal = program

    -- The rules.
    rule :: Target -> Rule
    rule t@(stem ++ ".o") =
      Rule t [stem ++ ".c"]
           (cc ++ " " ++ cflags ++ " -c " ++ stem ++ ".c -o " ++ t)
    rule t | t == library =
      Rule t libobjs (ar ++ " rcs " ++ t ++ " " ++ unwords libobjs)
    rule t | t == program =
      Rule t ins (cc ++ " -o " ++ t ++ " " ++ unwords ins)
      where ins = mainobjs ++ [library]
    rule "main.o" = Rule "main.o" ["shape.h"] ""

A rule is a value of the type Rule of the library: the target, its
prerequisites, and its command, one line as a makefile writes it.  A
makefile is a function from a target to its rule, and every equation that
matches a target is one answer.  The first equation is the pattern rule
%.o: %.c of make.  Its functional pattern stem ++ ".o" matches every object
name and binds the stem, so the rule names its source and its command from
the stem.  The as-pattern t@(...) names the whole target, as $@ does.  The
next two equations name their targets through the variables; the parameter
t stands where make writes $@, and ins where it writes $^.  The last
equation has no command: it is a prerequisite line, main.o: shape.h.  The
scanner of the driver finds that include as well; the line shows the form.

What a feature of a makefile maps to:

  * A variable, CC = cc, is a definition, cc = "cc".  Make writes CC;
    Curry keeps an uppercase name for a constructor, so the definition is
    lowercase.  The flags are one string, as in make.  A makefile that
    keeps them as a list joins them with unwords.
  * A pattern rule, %.o: %.c, is an equation with a functional pattern.
  * An explicit rule is an equation with a literal target or a guard on a
    variable.
  * A prerequisite line, main.o: shape.h, is a rule with the empty command.
    The library merges the prerequisites of every rule of a target, as
    make does, and takes the command from the rule that has one.
  * The automatic variables $@, $^, $< and $* are plain variables: the
    name t, the list ins, the source stem ++ ".c", the bound stem.
  * The automatic dependencies that make reads from the .d files of the
    compiler are a table that the driver scans: the headers each file
    includes.
  * make -n is the plan.  make -j is the ready set.  make CC=clang is a
    parameter of the rules, bound once per makefile (the toolchain, below).
  * The tie-break of make among several rules for one target is a lint
    here: the build does not start.
  * The stop of make on a source that is missing, "No rule to make
    target", is a question of the build, asked before the plan.

Not covered: phony targets, chains of pattern rules, VPATH, recursive make,
the built-in implicit rules of make, order-only prerequisites, and a target
without a command, which make rebuilds by doing nothing.  A name with no
rule, or with prerequisite lines only, is a source here.

Each question of a build is one function of Make.curry.  Each function
states a relation and a policy.

Which rules does a target have?  The set function rulesOf collects every
answer of the makefile as a list:

    rulesOf :: Makefile -> Target -> [Rule]
    rulesOf mk t = sortValues (set1 mk t)

No rule means a source, one rule with a command is the normal case, and two
rules with commands are the ambiguity below.  Everything else in the library
reads the rules through this list, for a reason that is a finding of this
example (#86).  A name that a functional pattern produced carries its
narrowing, and every later comparison of that name pays for it again.  A
first version of the engine read the rules from the pattern rule directly;
its plan over six targets took 10 s on the C++ backend.  Read through the
set function, whose values are plain data, the plan takes 0.01 s.  So the
functional pattern stays where it carries the meaning, in the makefile, and
the relation reads the rules as values.

The build.  make reads a makefile into its database before it builds
(make -p prints it).  The library does the same: build reads the makefile
once, from the goal down, into the rules of every name of the build, with
the automatic dependencies beside them:

    build :: Makefile -> Includes -> Target -> Build
    build mk inc goal = Build inc (reverse (snd (visit ([], []) goal)))
      where
        visit (seen, done) t
          | t `elem` seen = (seen, done)
          | otherwise     = (seen', (t, rs) : done')
          where rs = rulesOf mk t
                (seen', done') = foldl visit (t : seen, done) (inputsOf inc rs)

The names of the build are the goal, what it reads, what that reads, and so
on.  A name comes after what it reads, a name with no rule is a source, and
the list seen stops a cycle of rules.  The targets are the names with a
command.  Every question below reads the build and never the makefile.  The
makefile is read once per build, through twelve set functions here, and the
driver shares one build across its questions (section 2).

What does a target read?  The relation inputs gives the direct inputs of a
target: the prerequisites of every rule of it, each with the headers it
includes, directly or through another header.  The includes come from the
table that the driver scanned.  A header that includes a header is followed
through the table, and a list of the files seen stops a cycle of includes,
as an include guard does.  A source reads nothing.

What is stale?  The relation stale is over a table of stamps that the driver
supplies, a list of (file, stamp) pairs with one integer per file that
exists.  A target is stale when it is missing, when it is older than an
input, or when an input is stale:

    stale b table = staleFrom b table []

    staleFrom b table seen t
      | t `elem` seen = False
      | otherwise     = case lookup t table of
          Nothing -> True
          Just s  -> any (\i -> staleFrom b table (t : seen) i
                                  || maybe False (s <) (lookup i table))
                         (inputs b t)

A missing file has no entry, so a missing target is stale.  Curry compares
stamps and nothing else.  The list seen holds the targets on the path from
the question to this file: a target on its own path reads nothing more, so
a cycle of rules does not send the question round for ever.

What to build, and in which order?  The plan is the stale targets in
dependency order: waves lays them out in layers, and a layer holds the stale
targets whose stale inputs are all in earlier layers.  The policy is a
topological order over the relation.  A cycle leaves targets that never
enter a layer; the guard of layers fails then, and the plan has no value.  A
cycle is a failure, not an error.  The makefile project/Makefile_cycle.curry
adds one rule, shape.h <- demo, and the plan of a fresh copy has no value
(step 7 of the output).  Make drops a circular dependency with a warning and
builds on; here the question has no answer, and the driver says so.  Each
entry of the plan is a Rule again: the target, what it reads, and its
command.

What can run now?  The ready set is the stale targets whose inputs are all
up to date:

    ready b table =
      [ t | t <- targets b, stale b table t
          , not (any (stale b table) (inputs b t)) ]

The driver runs them in parallel, records the new stamps, and asks again.
The scheduler of the build is this one line of Curry.

What does a change touch?  The inverse query affected is the relation of
inputs read backwards.  The target is the unknown, drawn from the targets
with anyOf, and the guard asks whether it reads the file, directly or
through another target:

    affected b f | reads b t f = t
      where t = anyOf (targets b)

Each answer is one value.  The question "what rebuilds if shape.h changes"
needs no second relation and no index from files to targets.

Which targets do two rules claim?  The lint ambiguous lists the rules with
a command of every target that has more than one.  Make has a tie-break rule
for that case, which you have to remember: an explicit rule wins over a
pattern rule, and among pattern rules the shortest stem wins, then the first
rule in the makefile.  Here two rules for one target are two answers to one
question, and the driver reports them and refuses to build.  The makefile
project/Makefile_ambiguous.curry imports the makefile and adds one rule:

    rule t = Makefile.rule t
    rule "main.o" = Rule "main.o" ["main.c"] (cc ++ " -O3 -c main.c -o main.o")

Both equations match main.o, so main.o has two rules with a command.

Which sources are missing?  A source is a name without a command, and a
missing source has no stamp, so nothing can make it.  Make stops before it
builds: "No rule to make target 'vec.c', needed by 'vec.o'".  The question
missing lists each such source with the targets that read it:

    missing b table =
      [ (s, [t | t <- targets b, s `elem` inputs b t])
      | s <- names b, s `notElem` targets b, s `notElem` map fst table ]

The driver asks it before the plan, beside the lint, and refuses to build
(step 8 of the output).

The toolchain, and what call-time choice means for a makefile.  A makefile
that can use gcc or clang is the natural next rule, and it is where the
semantics of a choice shows.  Three forms were measured.

The first is the choice inside the rules, project/Makefile_choice.curry:

    cc :: String
    cc = "gcc" ? "clang"

    rule t@(stem ++ ".o") =
      Rule t [stem ++ ".c"]
           (cc ++ " " ++ cflags ++ " -c " ++ stem ++ ".c -o " ++ t)

A nullary definition is a function of no arguments.  Every use of it is a
new evaluation and a new choice, so every rule that reads cc picks a
compiler on its own.  The set function that collects the rules of a target
finds two rules for every target that reads cc, and the lint reports the
five of them (step 6 of the output).  Call-time choice shares the value of
a variable; it does not share a definition across its uses.

The second binds the compiler once.  rulesWith :: String -> Target -> Rule
takes it as a parameter, and one application rulesWith tc serves every rule
of the build.  Decided before the first set function starts, by a guard
tc =:= ("gcc" ? "clang") on a free variable or by a let that seq forces, the
choice gives one value per compiler for each question, consistent across
the rules (measured on the rules of two targets: two values, one compiler
each).  A plain let, let mk = rulesWith ("gcc" ? "clang") in ..., shares mk
across the questions as well.  But its choice reaches the first set
function undecided, inside the function that the set function applies, and
Sprite encapsulates a choice it meets that way: both compilers land in one
set, and the lint reports them.  That is the captured choice of #61, the
reading that Sprite's tests pin today.  The forms that decide the choice
first do not depend on that reading.  The form that works is also the form
that costs.  Every set function of the build meets the decided choice
again, and the price grows with the depth of the question.  Measured on the
C++ backend with the let and seq form: the names of the build 0.05 s, the
targets 0.5 s, the ready set 0.9 s, the waves not finished within a budget
of 60 s for the whole probe.  With the guard form: the names 0.12 s, the
targets 1.3 s, the waves not finished either.  On the Python backend the
names alone took 5.7 s and 11.9 s.  A choice between two whole makefiles,
rulesWith "gcc" ? rulesWith "clang", ran out of memory under the 2 GB cap
on the C++ backend in the names of the build.  This is the shape of #86
again, a narrowed node met again in a deep lazy context, and it is recorded
in TODO.

The third form is the one the example uses, project/Makefile_toolchain.curry:

    toolchains :: [String]
    toolchains = ["gcc", "clang"]

    rulesWith :: String -> Target -> Rule
    rulesWith cc t@(stem ++ ".o") = ...
    rulesWith _  t | t == library = Makefile.rule t
    rulesWith cc t | t == program = ...

    makefiles :: [(String, Makefile)]
    makefiles = [(cc, rulesWith cc) | cc <- toolchains]

The compiler is a parameter of the rules, bound once per makefile, and the
makefile lists one makefile per compiler.  The driver plans each of them
(step 5 of the output: two plans, one compiler each) and builds with the
first whose programs are on the PATH.  The lesson for a makefile is where
the choice lives.  A choice inside a rule is a choice per rule.  A choice
bound once is a choice per build, which is what "CC = gcc or clang" means.
And in Sprite today a choice that the questions of a build must meet again
is better bound by the driver than left to the runtime.


2. What the example shows about calling Curry from Python
----------------------------------------------------------

The driver make.py is generic for a C project.  It reads

    python make.py [-f FILE] [-C DIR] [-j N] [-n] [--touch FILE]
                   [--affected FILE] [--trace] [GOAL]
    python make.py [-f FILE] [-C DIR] [-j N] --serve PORT

as make does: -C names the project directory, -f the makefile in it
(Makefile.curry by default), -n prints the plan and stops, -j sets the width
of the thread pool, and GOAL the target, with the definition goal of the
makefile as the default.  --touch FILE gives a file a stamp newer than every
other file of the build before anything else, and --affected FILE prints the
targets that read the file.  --trace prints each ready set to stderr.

The makefile is a Curry module.  make.py puts its directory on curry.path
and imports it with curry.import_, so a makefile may import the modules
beside it, as the variants import Makefile.  The module exports rule, a
function, and the driver passes it to Curry as a value: a symbol with fewer
arguments than it takes is a partial application in curry.expr, and a
nullary symbol a call.  A module may export makefiles instead, a list of
named makefiles; the converter returns each as a Curry node, which the
driver passes on unchanged.

One build, many questions.  The driver builds the Curry value of the build
once,

    self.build = curry.expr(Make.build, rule, world.includes(), goal)

and passes the same node to every goal.  A node passes through curry.expr
untouched, and the first goal that needs the rules evaluates them in place,
so the makefile is read once per process: twelve set functions, 0.01 s on
the C++ backend and 0.2 s on the Python backend.  The table of stamps is a
separate argument, because it changes after every command.

A deterministic goal has one value, and the driver reads it with

    next(curry.eval(goal, *args, converter='topython'), None)

The converter turns a list of names into a list of str and a table into a
list of tuples.  A Rule is a data type of the library, which the converter
leaves as a Curry node, so the driver reads a list of rules through
Make.rows, which turns each rule into a tuple of a String, a list of
Strings and a String:

    curry.eval(
        Make.rows, curry.expr(Make.plan, build, table), converter='topython'
      )

The plan has no value on a cycle, and the driver reads None then and reports
the failure.  The inverse query is non-deterministic, and the driver
collects its values with sorted(curry.eval(...)), because Curry promises no
order.

The world.  Python scans each .c and .h file of the directory for quoted
includes with a regular expression, reads the modification time of each
file of the build in nanoseconds as its stamp, runs the commands, and serves
the status.  Curry gets the includes and the table as data and returns
names and rules.  The IO surface of Sprite is small, and a build needs none
of it: every decision is a function of the data Python has in hand.

A build lints first.  A target that two rules claim, or a source that is
missing, stops the build before the plan.  The build loop then asks Curry
for the ready set, submits each new ready target to a concurrent.futures
thread pool, waits for the first completion, and asks again with the new
stamps.  The table it hands to Curry omits the targets whose commands run
or failed.  A compiler creates its output before it finishes, and a
half-written object with a fresh stamp would read as up to date; a target
that is missing to Curry is stale, and its dependents are not ready.
Python keeps the running set and the outcome of each command, and nothing
of the dependency relation: no order, no count of pending inputs.  Run
make.py with --trace to see each ready set on stderr.  With -j 2 the
library was ready while report.o and main.o were still running, because
its two objects were done.  The waves in the output are the static picture
of the same relation.

A command runs as one process in the project directory, without a shell.
The driver splits the line into words as a shell would (shlex), so a quoted
argument stays one word.  When a program of the plan is not on the PATH,
every command runs as a stand-in that writes a placeholder file in place of
the target, so the example runs on a machine without a compiler and on CI.
The output is the same in both modes; the stand-in says so on stderr.  The
status table and the plan print no stamps, no times, and no temporary
paths, and the driver sorts what the thread pool returns.  The exit status
is that of make: 2 when the build does not start, when a command fails, or
when a target of the plan never ran, and 0 otherwise.

The option --serve PORT serves a status page with http.server from the
standard library.  The page shows the status table, a build button, and a
touch button, and /status.json gives the targets with their inputs and
staleness, the plan, and the ready set as JSON.  Each request reads the
makefile against the current state of the directory and calls the same
functions as the tested run.


3. How to run
-------------

Run "make stage" from the root of the repository first.  Then run

    ./run

The run script uses the C++ backend by default.  To pick a backend, set
SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS in the environment:

    SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS=backend:py ./run
    SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS=backend:cxx ./run

The script copies the sources of project/ into three temporary directories,
one of them without vec.c, and links the makefiles into each copy, so that
Sprite compiles each makefile once, into project/.curry, and the build
leaves nothing in the repository.  It runs make.py eight times there.

To build in project/ itself, run make.py from this directory:

    ../../install/bin/python make.py -C project
    ../../install/bin/python make.py -C project -n
    ../../install/bin/python make.py -C project --trace -j 2
    ../../install/bin/python make.py -C project -f Makefile_toolchain.curry -n

and remove the objects, the library and the program afterwards.  To serve
the status page, run

    ../../install/bin/python make.py -C project --serve 8000

and open http://127.0.0.1:8000/ in a browser.  Press Ctrl-C to stop it.
The run script does not start the server.


4. Expected output
------------------

The file expected.out holds the exact output of ./run.  Step 1 is the full
build:

    1. the full build
    % python make.py -C project
    plan: 6 targets
      main.o    <- main.c shape.h vec.h       cc -O2 -Wall -c main.c -o main.o
      report.o  <- report.c vec.h             cc -O2 -Wall -c report.c -o report.o
      vec.o     <- vec.c vec.h                cc -O2 -Wall -c vec.c -o vec.o
      shape.o   <- shape.c shape.h vec.h      cc -O2 -Wall -c shape.c -o shape.o
      libgeom.a <- vec.o shape.o              ar rcs libgeom.a vec.o shape.o
      demo      <- main.o report.o libgeom.a  cc -o demo main.o report.o libgeom.a
    waves: [main.o report.o vec.o shape.o] [libgeom.a] [demo]
    built: demo libgeom.a main.o report.o shape.o vec.o

Each object reads its source and the headers behind it: shape.o reads vec.h
through shape.h.  The targets come in the order of the build, from the goal
down, each after what it reads.  The four objects form the first wave, the
library the second, the program the third.  A status table follows, with
one line per target.  Step 2 builds again, and the plan is "nothing to do".
Step 3 touches shape.h:

    3. touch shape.h: the inverse query predicts the rebuild
    % python make.py -C project --touch shape.h --affected shape.h
    affected: demo libgeom.a main.o shape.o
    plan: 4 targets
    ...
    built: demo libgeom.a main.o shape.o

The inverse query predicts the four targets that read shape.h, and the
build then rebuilds exactly those.  vec.o and report.o stay up to date.
Step 4 reads the makefile with the second rule for main.o, and the lint
stops the build:

    4. a second rule for main.o: the lint
    % python make.py -C project -f Makefile_ambiguous.curry
    ambiguous: main.o has 2 rules
      main.o <- main.c  cc -O2 -Wall -c main.c -o main.o
      main.o <- main.c  cc -O3 -c main.c -o main.o
    refused: the build does not start
    exit status 2

The exit status is 2, as make's is on an error; the run script prints it.
Step 5 plans the toolchain makefile in a fresh copy, where everything is
stale, and prints one plan per compiler:

    5. the compiler left open, in a fresh copy: one plan per compiler
    % python make.py -C fresh -f Makefile_toolchain.curry -n
    makefile gcc:
    plan: 6 targets
      main.o    <- main.c shape.h vec.h       gcc -O2 -Wall -c main.c -o main.o
    ...
    makefile clang:
    plan: 6 targets
      main.o    <- main.c shape.h vec.h       clang -O2 -Wall -c main.c -o main.o
    ...

Step 6 plans the makefile with the choice inside the rules, and the lint
reports two rules for each of the five targets that read cc:

    6. the compiler as a choice inside the rules: every use picks on its own
    % python make.py -C fresh -f Makefile_choice.curry -n
    ambiguous: demo has 2 rules
      demo <- main.o report.o libgeom.a  clang -o demo main.o report.o libgeom.a
      demo <- main.o report.o libgeom.a  gcc -o demo main.o report.o libgeom.a
    ambiguous: main.o has 2 rules
    ...
    refused: the build does not start
    exit status 2

Step 7 plans the makefile with the rule that closes a cycle, in the fresh
copy, where every target of the cycle is stale:

    7. a rule that closes a cycle: the plan has no value
    % python make.py -C fresh -f Makefile_cycle.curry -n
    plan: no value (the rules have a cycle)
    exit status 2

Step 8 builds the copy without vec.c, and the build stops before the plan,
as make stops:

    8. a source that is missing: no rule to make it
    % python make.py -C incomplete
    no rule to make vec.c, needed by vec.o
    refused: the build does not start
    exit status 2

The output is identical on both backends, and with and without a C
compiler.


5. Limits
---------

Warm runs of ./run took 2.7 s on the C++ backend (three runs, 2.68 s to
2.79 s) and 11 s on the Python backend (two runs, 10.8 s and 11.1 s).  A
first run, which also converts the library and the five makefiles to
ICurry, took 6.2 s on the C++ backend.  Without a C compiler the run took
2.7 s on the C++ backend.  The run starts eight processes.  One process
with -n takes 0.3 s on the C++ backend and 1.0 s on the Python backend; the
import of curry and the library is 0.2 s of that, and the questions of the
plan are the rest.

Those times are with every Curry module interpreted on the C++ backend.
The runtime compiles a module in the background and kills that compile when
the process ends, and a make.py process ends first, so neither a makefile
nor the library gets its object from a run.  The command

    ../../install/bin/sprite-make --so Make.curry

writes the object of the library in 1.7 s; with it the run took 2.3 s (two
runs, 2.28 s and 2.34 s).

A name that a functional pattern produced carries its narrowing, and every
later comparison of that name pays for it again (#86).  A first version of
the engine read the rules from the pattern rule directly.  Its plan after
the touch did not finish in 120 s on either backend with the object name as
a functional pattern, and took 10 s on the C++ backend with a plain object
name.  Read through the set function rulesOf, whose values are plain data,
it takes 0.01 s.  A makefile that carries a choice shows the same shape at
the next level up, with the numbers of section 1: the questions of the build
meet the decided choice again in every set function, and the plan did not
finish within the budget of the probe.  The example binds the choice as a
list of makefiles for that reason.

A stamp is an integer that Python computes, here the modification time in
nanoseconds.  A target with the same stamp as an input is up to date, as in
make.  --touch waits until the file is newer than every other file of the
build, so a file system with a coarse clock gives the same output after a
pause of one tick.  A content hash is also an integer, but "older than" has
no meaning for it; the relation would then ask whether the stamp differs
from a recorded one.

The waves are the static picture: a layer holds the targets that can run
once the earlier layers are done.  The pool follows the dynamic ready set,
so a target of the second wave may start while the first wave still runs.
A target whose command fails stays stale, and its dependents are never
ready; the status table shows them as stale, and the exit status is 2.  A
command that writes no output counts as run, as in make, but its target is
still missing: the dependents never become ready, and the exit status is 2.
A source that is missing stops the build before the plan, with exit status
2 (step 8).  The plan sees a cycle when a
target of the cycle is stale; a cycle among targets that are up to date is
nothing to do.  Make reports a cycle before it looks at a stamp; that check
would be one more question, layers over every target.

The project is a toy by design: a handful of sources, one library, one
program.  The rules of Sprite's own Makefiles are not modelled, and Curry
does not replace GNU make at scale.  A real project would scan the includes
with the compiler's own dependency output and would need the features that
section 1 lists as not covered.  The relation does not change; only the
table that Python supplies grows.
